 ## Queue and Tickets
 
+### Queue
+
+A **queue** belongs to one entity. It has a `name`, a `slug` unique within the entity, an `is_active` flag (default `true`) and optional `labels_i18n` (per-locale labels). An inactive queue accepts no new tickets.
+
 ### Ticket
 
-Unit of service in the queue. It is **single-use** and receives **one single call** — all data is immutable after issuance, except `called_at` and `called_by` (filled when called). Attributes:
+Unit of service in the queue. It is **single-use** and receives **one single call** — all data is immutable after issuance, except `status`, `called_at` and `called_by` (filled when called). Attributes:
 
 - Source queue — the ticket belongs to **one** queue (within the entity); does not record which totem it came from.
+- Display code — the number the customer receives (see below).
 - Priority (numeric, see below).
 - Issuance timestamp (ordering "oldest first" within same priority).
-- Issuance channel: totem, virtual, printed copy, etc.
+- Issuance channel: `totem`, `virtual` or `printed`.
+- Status: `waiting` or `called`. There is no cancelled status.
+
+#### Display code
+
+- Format: `A` followed by at least three digits, zero padded (`A001`, `A002`, … `A999`); beyond 999 it grows (`A1000`, `A1001`).
+- Unique per `(queue, code)`.
+- The next code is the last well-formed code in **insertion order** plus one, so `A999` followed by `A1000` yields `A1001`. Insertion order, not string order, decides the "last" code.
+- Malformed codes (anything not matching `A<digits>`) are skipped: with only `A-X` present, the next code is `A001`; with `A001` then `A-X`, the next code is `A002`.
+- Deleting a ticket from the middle of the sequence leaves a gap and does not reuse its number: after `A001`..`A003` with `A002` deleted, the next code is `A004`. Deleting the most recently issued ticket makes its number the next one again, because the next code is derived from the tickets that remain.
+
+#### Issuance
+
+- A new ticket is `waiting`, with `issued_at` set to now, channel `virtual` and priority `0` by default.
+- A negative priority is rejected.
+- Issuing into an inactive queue is rejected. The queue state is read from the database while the queue row is locked, not from the caller's instance, so a queue deactivated after the caller loaded it is still rejected.
+- A rejected issuance persists nothing.
+- A code collision with a concurrent issuance is retried a few times before failing.
+
+#### Write-once and single call
+
+- After issuance, every field except `status`, `called_at`, `called_by_user_id` and `updated_at` is immutable. Any attempt to change another field is rejected.
+- A ticket moves from `waiting` to `called` once. The transition records `called_at` and the calling user. A non-waiting ticket cannot be called.
+- Only members of the ticket's entity may call it, in any role (owner, admin or employee). Anyone else is rejected and the ticket is unchanged.
+- Choosing *which* ticket to call next (priority and fairness) is covered by the ordering rules below, not by the single-call rule.
 
